Host-side gtest unit tests for crosspoint-reader.

Build and run:

  cmake -S test -B build/test
  cmake --build build/test
  ctest --test-dir build/test --output-on-failure -j

Run a single suite directly:

  cmake --build build/test --target StreamingJsonParserTest
  build/test/streaming_json_parser/StreamingJsonParserTest --gtest_filter='*'

Google Test is fetched via CMake FetchContent on first configure; the pinned
version lives in test/CMakeLists.txt.

Shell-script based tests (suites that cannot be built with the host CMake
toolchain due to embedded-only dependencies):

  bash test/run_epub_css_test.sh

KOReader sync (lib/KOReaderSync) is esp_http_client-based and cannot be built
by this toolchain either. It is covered instead by a conformance harness that
speaks the firmware's exact wire format to a live KOSync server:

  # Offline. No network, no account -- pins the status -> Error mapping table.
  python scripts/kosync_conformance.py --self-test

  # Online. Auth + progress round-trip against a real server.
  python scripts/kosync_conformance.py --server https://sync.koreader.rocks \
      --user NAME --password PASS

The default online run creates no account and writes progress only under a
fixed, namespaced test document id. Account registration is opt-in behind
--check-register-existing (one create attempt for an account you already own)
and --allow-create-user (creates one, requires an explicit fixed username).
See the module docstring for why those are gated.

The mapping table in the harness is a hand transcription of
KOReaderSyncClient.cpp; there is no automatic link, so change both together.
